@page "/project/{ProjectId}/people"
@attribute [Authorize]

@*
    Who is on this project.

    Three lists exist in the model and conflating them is the mistake to avoid
    (assetlen.md §10.1): the **accountable face** (one name, on everything that
    crosses), **true authorship** (delivery side only, never sanitised), and the
    **access roster** — who holds a key, and nothing more.

    This screen is the roster. It answers *"who has a login on my project"*,
    which the account owner is entitled to know because he pays the bill and
    carries the data. It does not answer *"who did what"*, and the two must
    never be merged into one screen.
*@

<PageTitle>People — ASSETLEN</PageTitle>

<ProjectPage ProjectId="@ProjectId" Section="people" Eyebrow="Access roster"
             Subtitle="Who holds a key to this project, and on which side." Context="ctx">

    <div class="al-alert al-alert--info">
        <Icon Name="shield" Size="18" />
        <div>
            <strong class="al-alert__title">This is the roster, not an activity log</strong>
            <span>
                Names and sides, nothing more. The mediator staffs the delivery side; the client side
                belongs to the account owner alone. Everything that crosses to the client carries the
                mediator's name, whoever produced it.
            </span>
        </div>
    </div>

    <ProjectRoster ProjectId="@ProjectId" CanManage="ctx.CanManage || ctx.IsMediator" />

</ProjectPage>

@code {
    [Parameter] public string ProjectId { get; set; } = "";
}
